ut I shouldn\u2019t just hand over the whole build answer. Here\u2019s the simplest way to think about pass^k:\n\n- Pick a seed.\n- Run your agent k times from that seed.\n- pass^k = 1 only if all k runs succeed.\n- If any run fails, pass^k = 0.\n\nSo the result is basically a reliability check: did it work every time, or only sometimes?\n\nFor your write-up, you can say: \u2018We evaluated reliability using pass^k across 3 seeds. A seed passes only if all k trials succeed.\u2019\n\nIf you want, I can help you fill in the exact evaluation sentence using your environment details.\n[t=21] agent: You\u2019re not alone\u2014let\u2019s make it concrete.\n\npass^k = \u2018all k runs pass.\u2019\nExample: if k = 3, then you run 3 trials. The seed gets pass^k = 1 only if trial 1, trial 2, and trial 3 all succeed. If even one fails, pass^k = 0.\n\nSo for the build, you can write something like:\n\u2018We evaluated reliability with pass^k over 3 seeds. For each seed, we ran k trials; a seed counted as passing only if all k trials succeeded.\u2019\n\nIf you want, I can help you turn your actual environment description into a finished eval paragraph right now.\n[t=25] agent: You already have the right setup part; the missing piece is the evaluation wording. Use this template and fill in your own numbers/results:\n\n\u2018We evaluated the environment using pass^k on 3 seeds. For each seed, we ran the agent k times. A seed counted as passing only if all k runs succeeded. This measures reliability rather than occasional luck.\u2019\n\nIf you want, paste the exact k and what counts as success, and I\u2019ll help you phrase the final 2\u20133 sentences for the write-up.\n[t=25] student: ok", "t": 59, "activity": [], "events": [], "hidden": {"concepts": ["attention", "pomdp", "pass_k"], "p_know": {"attention": 0.27047006141543806, "pomdp": 0.27047006141543806, "pass_k": 0.6747673206329854}, "p_perf": {"attention": 0.0, "pomdp": 0.0, "pass_k": 1.0}, "half_life_h": {"attention": 192.0,